```java
@Service
public class BankTransferService {
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    @Transactional
    public void transferMoney(Long fromId, Long toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Repository methods use SELECT ... FOR UPDATE in a consistent id order.
        var from = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new AccountNotFoundException(fromId));
        var to = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new AccountNotFoundException(toId));

        from.debit(amount);       // checks the encoded balance rule
        to.credit(amount);
    } // the transaction manager attempts one commit; either both changes persist or neither does
}
```

The boundary supplies atomicity for the two database changes. The locking query and debit check supply concurrency control and the business rule; `@Transactional` does not invent either one.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest request) {
        var order = orderRepo.save(new Order(request.customerId()));
        inventoryRepo.reserveIfAvailable(request.items());
        return order;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true) // intent hint; effect depends on manager/provider
    public Optional<Order> findOrder(Long orderId) {
        return orderRepo.findById(orderId);
    }
}
```

This forms one transaction only when both repositories use the participating transactional resource. A default rollback exception must escape for the first method to roll back automatically.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;
    private final InventoryRepository inventoryRepo;

    // No @Transactional: each save() is its own transaction.
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);                 // committed immediately
        inventoryRepo.decrementStock(order);   // if THIS throws...
        // ...the order is already saved, but stock was never reduced.
        // Result: an order exists for inventory you didn't deduct.
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;
    private final InventoryRepository inventoryRepo;

    @Transactional  // one transaction wraps both writes
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);                 // not yet committed
        inventoryRepo.decrementStock(order);   // not yet committed
    }   // proxy commits here on success, or rolls back if either throws
}
```

*If `decrementStock` throws a `RuntimeException`, Spring rolls back the order insert too — there is no orphaned order.*

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        int changed = inventoryRepo.reserveIfAvailable(
                order.getItemId(), order.getQty());
        if (changed != 1) {
            throw new OutOfStockException(order.getItemId());
        }
        orderRepo.save(order);
    }
}
```

The transaction keeps the successful stock reservation and order insert in one unit. The conditional update prevents stock from dropping below the encoded limit; the transaction boundary alone would not prevent that race.

```sql
-- PostgreSQL: set the level when the transaction starts.
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;

SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;
-- Later plain SELECT statements use the same transaction snapshot.
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;

COMMIT;
```

This example describes PostgreSQL. Other engines may use different snapshots or locks at the same named level.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    // Joins the caller's transaction or starts one.
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
    public void pay(String requestId, Order order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);
        auditService.recordAttempt(requestId);
    }
}

@Service
public class AuditService {
    // Separate transaction; the record must not reference an uncommitted order row.
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void recordAttempt(String requestId) {
        auditRepo.save(new AuditEntry(requestId, "PAYMENT_ATTEMPT"));
    }
}
```

The audit commit is independent of a later outer rollback. In practice, decide how an audit failure propagates and size the connection pool for the extra transaction.
